olivia.chen: Hi! Quick onboarding q — where should I store a REDWOOD_HOSTED_API_KEY for CI vs local dev? Also, is it ok to use the sandbox key in CI for our end-to-end streaming tests?

samir.patel: welcome olivia! short answer: never check keys into the repo. :wave: For CI use the org-level secret store (GitHub Actions secrets / Vault). For local dev use the sandbox key.

olivia.chen: got it. any naming conventions for the secret in GH actions? and what about quotas — our e2e streaming tests open a long-lived stream for ~2-3 minutes each, worried about hitting rate limits in sandbox.

samir.patel: name wise we usually do REDWOOD_HOSTED_API_KEY for prod-like flows and REDWOOD_SANDBOX_KEY for dev. For CI we create a repo secret pointing to the hosted key if tests need production-ish throughput. Sandbox keys have low quotas and are meant for interactive dev, so you'll likely hit 429s with long streaming runs.

jordan.lee: security note: prefer short-lived credentials if possible. For CI we recommend using our internal credential broker (request via #devex with the template) which provisions a scoped token that rotates and is vaulted. If you need to run in GH Actions without the broker, use org secrets + limit who can edit workflows. Do not echo keys in logs.

olivia.chen: how do I request the scoped token / quota bump?

devex-bot: To request CI tokens or sandbox quota increases, file a ticket at https://redwood-internal/support with: repo name, test cadence, approximate token usage (tokens/min), and justification. Typical turnaround 1-2 business days.

samir.patel: and for the streaming 429s: implement exponential backoff + jitter. here's a tiny JS snippet you can drop into test harness for retrying on 429:

```js
async function callWithBackoff(fn) {
  let attempt = 0;
  while (attempt < 6) {
    try {
      return await fn();
    } catch (err) {
      if (err.status !== 429) throw err;
      const wait = Math.min(1000 * 2 ** attempt, 10000) + Math.floor(Math.random() * 300);
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, wait));
      attempt++;
    }
  }
  throw new Error('max retries reached');
}
```

elaine.park: if you absolutely need deterministic CI for E2E streaming (no retries), request a short-lived hosted test token + quota bump from devex and mark the workflow as "runs-on: self-hosted" on infra-approved runners to avoid noisy shared buckets.

olivia.chen: ah ok — so options are: 1) run cheap/short tests with sandbox in CI (flaky), 2) use hosted key in secrets + accept possible throttles, 3) ask devex for scoped token + quota bump and run on approved runners. correct?

samir.patel: correct. Also add instrumentation to record 429 counts so we can tune quotas if you open a ticket. The console has per-token rate charts.

jordan.lee: one more: rotate keys on schedule and audit usage. If you use vault/broker we handle rotation automatically. If you keep static secrets in GH, rotate every 90 days.

olivia.chen: perfect — I'll file the support ticket with test cadence and token estimates. Thanks y'all :pray:

samir.patel: ping me on the ticket if you want a fast review of the GH Actions setup.

olivia.chen: will do. thanks!
